## 2. GGUF Split Paths: Symlink Resolution on Hugging Face Snapshots

**Component:** `include/strata/artifact/gguf_split.hpp` — `gguf_split_paths()`

**Responsibilities:** Resolves a model's shard list from any shard's path by rewriting the five-digit shard number. Throws when a shard is missing.

**Problem:** Hugging Face snapshot directories store the real blobs under hash names and create symlinks with the split names. `std::ifstream(p)` follows symlinks on Linux but on Windows a junction or a hardlink created by `git lfs` may not be a regular file, and `std::ifstream` succeeds on a directory on some platforms. The current check is binary but does not verify the file is readable and non-empty, so a zero-byte placeholder (a failed download) passes the existence check and fails later at mmap with a confusing "data section starts past EOF".

**Proposed change:** After the `ifstream` open, check `file.tellg()` or use `std::filesystem::file_size` to reject a zero-byte shard with a message naming it and saying "the download is incomplete".

```diff
--- a/include/strata/artifact/gguf_split.hpp
+++ b/include/strata/artifact/gguf_split.hpp
@@ -1,6 +1,7 @@
 #pragma once

 #include <cstdio>
+#include <filesystem>
 #include <fstream>
 #include <stdexcept>
 #include <string>
@@ -38,8 +39,11 @@
         std::string p = any;
         p.replace(at - 5, 5, num);
-        if (!std::ifstream(p, std::ios::binary))
+        std::error_code ec;
+        const auto sz = std::filesystem::file_size(p, ec);
+        if (ec || sz == 0)
             throw std::runtime_error("missing model shard " + p + " (shard " + std::to_string(i) + " of " +
-                                     std::to_string(n) + "; is the download complete?)");
+                                     std::to_string(n) + (ec ? "; cannot open it" : "; it is empty - the download is incomplete") + ")");
         out.push_back(std::move(p));
```

**Verification cases:**
1. Create a directory with four shard names where shard 3 is a zero-byte file; `gguf_split_paths` throws naming shard 3 and "empty".
2. A valid four-shard set (each > 1 MB) resolves to four paths in order; a single-file model (no `-of-` suffix) returns a one-element vector.

